{
  "Date": "08/09/2026",
  "Title": "UPSC Civil Services Examination (CSE) Syllabus (Sociology)",
  "Category": "Sociology Syllabus",
  "Banner_Image": "https://drive.google.com/thumbnail?id=1SZtd-AEF20kHNMCW6wU3dKw4Ci-Vqlyv&sz=w1200",
  "Short_Summary": "A comprehensive guide to UPSC Sociology, covering key concepts, sociological thinkers, theories, and important topics relevant to the examination.\r\nHelps aspirants develop a strong understanding of Sociology and prepare systematically for both UPSC Prelims and Mains.\r",
  "Full_Content": "<p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-center\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>SOCIOLOGY </strong></p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-center\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>PAPER - 1</strong></p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-center\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 10px;\"><strong>FUNDAMENTALS OF SOCIOLOGY</strong></p><h3 class=\"text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold text-[#6C1D18] mt-6 text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>1.</strong>\t<strong>Sociology - The Discipline:</strong></h3><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(a)        Modernity and social changes in Europe and emergence of Sociology.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(b)   \tScope of the subject and comparison with other social sciences.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(c)        Sociology and common sense.</p><h3 class=\"text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold text-[#6C1D18] mt-6 text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>2.</strong>\t<strong>Sociology as Science:</strong></h3><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(a)        Science, scientific method and critique.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(b)   \tMajor theoretical strands of research methodology.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(c)        Positivism and its critique.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(d)   \tFact value and objectivity.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">( e) Non-positivist methodologies.</p><h3 class=\"text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold text-[#6C1D18] mt-6 text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>3.</strong>   <strong>Research Methods and Analysis:</strong></h3><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(a)        Qualitative and quantitative methods.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(b)   \tTechniques of data collection.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(c ) Variables, sampling, hypothesis, reliability and validity.</p><h3 class=\"text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold text-[#6C1D18] mt-6 text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>4.</strong>   <strong>Sociological Thinkers:</strong></h3><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(a)        Karl Marx - Historical materialism, mode of production, alienation, class struggle.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(b)   \tEmile Durkhteim - Division of labour, social fact, suicide, religion and society.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.38; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(c)        Max Weber - Social action, ideal types, authority, bureaucracy, protestant ethic and the spirit of capitalism.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(d)   \tTalcolt Parsons - Social system, pattern variables.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(e)        Robert K. Merton - Latent and manifest functions, conformity and deviance, reference groups.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(f)         Mead - Self and identity.</p><h3 class=\"text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold text-[#6C1D18] mt-6 text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>5.</strong>\t<strong>Stratification and Mobility :</strong></h3><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(a)        Concepts - equality, inequality, hierarchy, exclusion, poverty and deprivation.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(b)   \tTheories of social stratification - Structural func tionalist theory, Marxist theory, Weberian theory.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(c)   \tDimensions - Social stratification of class, status groups, gender, ethnicity and race.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(d)   \tSocial mobility - open and closed systems, types of mobility, sources and causes of mobility.</p><h3 class=\"text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold text-[#6C1D18] mt-6 text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>6.</strong>   <strong>Works and Economic Life :</strong></h3><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.4000000000000001; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(a)        Social organization of work in different types of society - slave society, feudal society, industrial capitalist society.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(b)   \tFormal and informal organization of work.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(c)        Labour and society.</p><h3 class=\"text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold text-[#6C1D18] mt-6 text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>7.</strong>\t<strong>Politics and Society:</strong></h3><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(a)        Sociological theories of power.</p><div style=\"height: 12px;\"></div><div style=\"height: 12px;\"></div><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(b)       Power elite, bureaucracy, pressure groups and political parties.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(c)        Nation, state, citizenship, democracy, civil society, ideology.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(d)       Protest, agitation, social movements, collective action, revolution.</p><h3 class=\"text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold text-[#6C1D18] mt-6 text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>8.</strong>   <strong>Religion and Society :</strong></h3><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(a)        Sociological theories of religion.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(b)       Types of religious practices: animism, monism, pluralism, sects, cults.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.38; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(c)      Religion in modern society: religion and science, secularization, religious revivalism, fundamen talism.</p><h3 class=\"text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold text-[#6C1D18] mt-6 text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>9.</strong>\t<strong>Systems of Kinship:</strong></h3><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(a)        Family, household, marriage.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(b)       Types and forms of family.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(c)        Lineage and descent.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(d)       Patriarchy and sexual division of labour.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(e)        Contemporary trends.</p><h3 class=\"text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold text-[#6C1D18] mt-6 text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>10.</strong>   <strong>Social Change in Modern Society :</strong></h3><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(a)        Sociological theories of social change.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(b)       Development and dependency.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(c)        Agents of social change.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(d)       Education and social change.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(e)        Science, technology and social change.</p><div style=\"height: 12px;\"></div><h2 class=\"text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif font-bold text-[#6C1D18] mt-8 text-center\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>PAPER–II</strong></h2><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-center\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 10px;\"><strong>INDIAN SOCIETY : STRUCTURE AND CHANGE</strong></p><li class=\"ml-4 md:ml-6 pl-2 list-disc text-stone-800 leading-relaxed text-base md:text-lg\" style=\"margin-bottom: 8px;\">Introducing Indian society: </li><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>(i)</strong>          <strong>Perspectives on the Study of Indian Society: </strong></p><li class=\"ml-4 md:ml-6 pl-2 list-disc text-stone-800 leading-relaxed text-base md:text-lg\" style=\"margin-bottom: 8px;\"><strong>Indology, (G.S Ghure)</strong></li><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(b)          Structural functionalism (M. N. Srinivas).</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(c)          Marxist sociology (A. R. Desai).</p><h3 class=\"text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold text-[#6C1D18] mt-6 text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>(ii)</strong>           <strong>Impact of colonial rule on Indian society :</strong></h3><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(a)          Social background of Indian nationalism.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(b)          Modernization of Indian tradition.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(c)          Protests and movements during the colonial period.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(d)          Social reforms.</p><h3 class=\"text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold text-[#6C1D18] mt-6 text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>B.</strong>   <strong>Social Structure:</strong></h3><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>(i)</strong>\t<strong>Rural and Agrarian Social Structure:</strong></p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(a)            The idea of Indian village and village studies.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(b)            Agrarian social structure— evolution of land tenure system, land reforms.</p><h3 class=\"text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold text-[#6C1D18] mt-6 text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>(ii)</strong>\t<strong>Caste System:</strong></h3><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.38; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(a)        Perspectives on the study of caste systems: G. S. Ghurye, M. N. Srinivas, Louis Dumont, Andre Beteille.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(b)   \tFeatures of caste system.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(c)   \tUntouchability-forms and perspectives</p><h3 class=\"text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold text-[#6C1D18] mt-6 text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>(iii)</strong> \t<strong>Tribal Communities in India:</strong></h3><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(a)        Definitional problems.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(b)   \tGeographical spread.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(c)   \tColonial policies and tribes.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(d)   \tIssues of integration and autonomy.</p><h3 class=\"text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold text-[#6C1D18] mt-6 text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>(iv)</strong> \t<strong>Social Classes in India:</strong></h3><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(a)        Agrarian class structure.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(b)   \tIndustrial class structure.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(c)   \tMiddle classes in India.</p><h3 class=\"text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold text-[#6C1D18] mt-6 text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>(v)</strong> \t<strong>Systems of Kinship in India:</strong></h3><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(a)        Lineage and descent in India.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(b)   \tTypes of kinship systems.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(c)   \tFamily and marriage in India.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(d)   \tHousehold dimensions of the family.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(e)   \tPatriarchy, entitlements and sexual division oflabour.</p><h3 class=\"text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold text-[#6C1D18] mt-6 text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>(vi)</strong> \t<strong>Religion and Society :</strong></h3><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(a)        Religious communities in India.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(b)   \tProblems of religious minorities.</p><h3 class=\"text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold text-[#6C1D18] mt-6 text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>C.</strong>   \t<strong>Social Changes in India:</strong></h3><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>(i)</strong>  \t<strong>Visions of Social Change in India:</strong></p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(a)        Idea of development planning and mixed economy.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(b)   \tConstitution, law and social change.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(c)       Education and social change.</p><h3 class=\"text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold text-[#6C1D18] mt-6 text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>(ii)</strong> \t<strong>Rural and Agrarian Transformation in India:</strong></h3><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.38; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(a)        Programmes of rural development, Community Development Programme, cooperatives, poverty alleviation schemes.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(b)   \tGreen revolution and social change.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(c)   \tChanging modes of production in Indian agriculture.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(d)   \tProblems of rural labour, bondage, migration.</p><div style=\"height: 12px;\"></div><div style=\"height: 12px;\"></div><h3 class=\"text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold text-[#6C1D18] mt-6 text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>(iii)</strong>     <strong>Industrialization and Urbanisation in India:</strong></h3><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(a)        Evolution of modern industry in India.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(b)   \tGrowth of urban settlements in India.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(c)   \tWorking class: structure, growth, class mobilization.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(d)   \tInformal sector, child labour.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(e)   \tSlums and deprivation in urban areas.</p><h3 class=\"text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold text-[#6C1D18] mt-6 text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>(iv)</strong> \t<strong>Politics and Society :</strong></h3><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(a)        Nation, democracy and citizenship.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(b)   \tPolitical parties, pressure groups, social and political elite.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(c)   \tRegionalism and decentralization of power.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(d)   \tSecularization.</p><h3 class=\"text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold text-[#6C1D18] mt-6 text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>(v)</strong> \t<strong>Social Movements in Modern India :</strong></h3><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(a)        Peasants and farmers movements.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(b)   \tWomen’s movement.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(c)   \tBackward classes &amp; Dalit movements.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(d)   \tEnvironmental movements.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(e)   \tEthnicity and Identity movements.</p><h3 class=\"text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold text-[#6C1D18] mt-6 text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>(vi)</strong> \t<strong>Population Dynamics :</strong></h3><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(a)        Population size, growth, composition and distribution.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(b)   \tComponents of population growth: birth, death, migration.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(c)   \tPopulation Policy and family planning.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(d)   \tEmerging issues: ageing, sex ratios, child and infant mortality, reproductive health.</p><h3 class=\"text-xl md:text-2xl font-serif font-semibold text-[#6C1D18] mt-6 text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\"><strong>(vii)</strong>         <strong>Challenges of Social Transformation :</strong></h3><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(a)        Crisis of development : displacement, environmental problems and sustainability.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(b)   \tPoverty, deprivation and inequalities.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(c)   \tViolence against women.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(d)   \tCaste conflicts.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(e)   \tEthnic conflicts, communalism, religious revivalism.</p><p class=\"text-stone-800 text-base md:text-lg text-left\" style=\"line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 12px;\">(f)   \tIlliteracy and disparities in education.</p><div style=\"height: 12px;\"></div><div style=\"height: 12px;\"></div><div style=\"height: 12px;\"></div><div style=\"height: 10px;\"></div><div style=\"height: 12px;\"></div>",
